cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Whole-document batches are smaller than they look: measuring within-document gradient correlation"
message: "If you use this code or data, please cite it as below."
type: dataset
version: "1.0"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.23268862"
date-released: "2026-10-09"
url: "https://severinvisionary.github.io/doc-batch-gradients/"
repository-code: "https://github.com/SeverinVisionary/doc-batch-gradients"
authors:
  - given-names: Hanyu
    family-names: Yang
    email: hanyu.yang.92@gmail.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-0419-4070"
abstract: >-
  A preregistered measurement (one control-relative rule added after part of the data was seen) of
  within-document gradient correlation on open language-model checkpoints
  (Pythia-410M, Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-2-1B). At equal tokens, a whole-document 16K-token batch has 3.5-4.9x fewer
  effective independent samples than packed web text for code repositories, about 2.3x fewer for arXiv papers
  and 1.3-1.6x fewer for PG-19 books. Chunks were run as standalone 512-token sequences, so this is a proxy for
  16K-context training; real long-context gradients were not measured.
keywords:
  - gradient noise
  - critical batch size
  - design effect
  - long-context training
  - language models
